TY  - RPRT
AU  - Aktuğ, Emrehan
AU  - Torul, Orhan
TI  - The Market-Perceived Monetary Policy Rule: Evidence from Prediction Markets
PY  - 2026
UR  - https://orhantorul.com/research/marper/
KW  - Monetary policy rule
KW  - perceived reaction function
KW  - prediction markets
KW  - high-frequency identification.
AB  - How strongly do markets expect the Federal Reserve to respond to economic news? We measure this market-perceived policy rule in real time using prediction markets. Kalshi prices both the coming CPI print and the next FOMC decision, so inflation news can be measured against the market’s own expectation minutes before the release, and the policy response read from prices within the same hour. Combined with fed funds futures and Kalshi contracts on later CPI prints, this simultaneous measurement links the expectation of today’s print, the policy response and expectations of future prints, rather than pairing a price response with a survey consensus collected days earlier. Over 2021–2026, a 1 percentage point upside CPI surprise raises the expected rate at the next FOMC meeting by 18 basis points, and other major releases move it with the signs a policy rule implies. Measured against the survey consensus instead, the same price changes imply a response a fifth to a quarter weaker. The response builds along the policy path, from 13 basis points at one month to about 72 at eleven months, and the same news raises the expected next CPI print. A hot print thus raises expected policy far beyond the next meeting and expected near-term inflation, all within the hour.
